WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
Two months before he died, Chris Farley&nbsp;
returned to host the show that made him famous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.840
It was hard to watch for all the wrong reasons.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.760
In 1995, two years before Farley's final&nbsp;
"Saturday Night Live" appearance, New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.040
Magazine published an article titled "Comedy Isn't&nbsp;
Funny," detailing the show's dysfunction behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.560
the scenes. To make matters worse, Farley graced&nbsp;
the magazine's cover, seemingly branding him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.120
the cause of the show's plummeting ratings.&nbsp;
The show was facing a PR nightmare, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.760
only solution was to clean house. "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" producer Lorne Michaels, whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.880
reputation was tarnished following the article,&nbsp;
had no choice but to completely revamp the show.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.840
Between 1994 and 1995, the show&nbsp;
dropped almost its entire cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.440
including some of its biggest stars&nbsp;
like Phil Hartman and Mike Myers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.840
Some cast members, like Chris Farley and Adam&nbsp;
Sandler, were fired at the end of the season.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.720
The firing of Farley and Sandler came as a&nbsp;
shock, particularly to the actors themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.080
Sandler told The Daily Beast. He&nbsp;
also detailed how the two used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.400
each other for emotional support during this time.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:09.520
"We got mad together, pretended- pretended we&nbsp;
weren't sad and pretended this was for the best."

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.760
However, Farley's time away from the show wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.120
be that long — although his return&nbsp;
would come with quite the price.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.320
Farley's time following "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;
was the start of a burgeoning movie career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.960
In '95 and '96, he and "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" castmate David Spade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.000
starred in the comedy films "Tommy Boy" and "Black&nbsp;
Sheep," both of which were beloved by audiences.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.480
However, not all of this success was appealing&nbsp;
to Farley, who for years had struggled with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.800
public image and drug abuse as a result of&nbsp;
his fame. According to the New York Post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.640
Farley was disappointed with "Black&nbsp;
Sheep" but nevertheless agreed to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.640
At a screening of the film in New&nbsp;
Jersey, Farley relapsed on alcohol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.600
after three years of sobriety. He returned to&nbsp;
rehab prior to filming "Beverly Hills Ninja,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.280
a movie his close friends and family&nbsp;
tried to convince him not to make.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.920
Farley returned to "Saturday Night&nbsp;
Live" in 1996 alongside David Spade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.720
to promote "Black Sheep." Over a&nbsp;
year later, on October 25, 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.280
Farley was invited to host the show. Unbeknownst&nbsp;
to the cast, crew, and audience at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.280
this hosting gig would be Farley's last&nbsp;
appearance on TV before he died in December 1997.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.240
The opening of Chris's "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;
hosting debut wasted no time in addressing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.280
struggles with substance abuse. The cold open&nbsp;
features Tim Meadows in Lorne Michaels' office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.080
trying to convince the producer to let Farley&nbsp;
host. Michaels is apprehensive about letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.400
Farley back, but Meadows reassures&nbsp;
him that post-rehab Farley is clean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.520
The trio is also visited by Farley's sponsor,&nbsp;
Chevy Chase, who backs up Farley's claim.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.960
Unfortunately, Farley's promise&nbsp;
to Lorne doesn't last long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.120
as the comedian doesn't show up for his&nbsp;
monologue only moments later. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.760
he's in his old dressing room, flirting&nbsp;
with cast member Ana Gasteyer. Tim Meadows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.640
tries to come to the rescue as a replacement&nbsp;
host until Michaels forces him off the stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.080
in place of Chris Rock. Together, Meadows and&nbsp;
Rock introduce Farley, and they start the show.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.920
For his first real sketch back since being fired&nbsp;
in 1995, Farley plays the producer of a morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:14.160
talk show called "Morning Latte." In this sketch,&nbsp;
Will Ferrell and Cheri Oteri's talk show hosts are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.440
hysterical over an anecdote of Farley's character&nbsp;
involving an embarrassing CD purchase he made.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.480
The sketch grows increasingly awkward as Farley's&nbsp;
producer, Ted, tries to match the energy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.680
Ferrell and Oteri's characters, and chimes in&nbsp;
that he also met Will Smith over the weekend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.040
They seem unfazed by this news, still cracking&nbsp;
jokes about his humiliating CD story. The sketch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:41.280
also contains an iconic Cheri Oteri moment as&nbsp;
she emphasizes Ferrell's Italian translations:

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.920
"Which uh, of course in Italian means-

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.040
"Affection!"

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.120
"No, actually it's abundance."

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:46.620
"Abundance!"

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.800
What really makes the sketch, however,&nbsp;
is Farley's somewhat subdued performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.440
It's refreshing to see the comedian playing a&nbsp;
character who seems almost frustrated with how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.160
over-the-top he has to be to earn the affections&nbsp;
of these naive daytime talk show personalities.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.200
Molly Shannon and Farley were only ever on&nbsp;
the cast together for half of one season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.000
but fortunately, the two got their chance to&nbsp;
shine together when Farley returned to host.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.520
In the second sketch of the night,&nbsp;
Shannon reprises her iconic character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.040
Mary Katherine Gallagher, an&nbsp;
unpopular Catholic school girl.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.720
The sketch takes place during a dance&nbsp;
at St. Monica's Catholic School.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.280
Farley, playing a student named Mickey,&nbsp;
asks the school's nuns to dance with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.000
as he doesn't have a date. Mary Katherine&nbsp;
Gallagher, on the other hand, fawns over another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.280
student, but when Mary's crush rejects her, she&nbsp;
finds herself being asked to dance by Mickey.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.080
This is certainly one of the&nbsp;
cuter sketches of the night and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.800
excellent showcase for both Farley and Shannon.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.800
It wouldn't be an episode of "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" hosted by Chris Farley without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.920
an appearance by the motivational speaker Matt&nbsp;
Foley. The character originated at Second City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.880
and was developed by Bob Odenkirk based on an&nbsp;
improvisation by Farley. The original sketch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.720
is often regarded as one of the greatest&nbsp;
sketches in "Saturday Night Live" history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.280
and the character of Matt Foley is easily one&nbsp;
of the most famous ever featured on the show.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.600
Farley reprised the character many times while he&nbsp;
was on the show, although this appearance would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.320
be his last time donning the iconic glasses&nbsp;
and checkered jacket. In this iteration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.840
Foley is hired as a motivational speaker for a&nbsp;
spin cycle class, screaming at the participants as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.040
he rips his pants and rants about living in a van&nbsp;
down by the river in an attempt to motivate them.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.360
The biggest laugh from the audience in this&nbsp;
sketch comes when Matt Foley needs to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.240
break to hydrate. He pulls out a coffee pot from&nbsp;
his duffel bag and douses himself in coffee.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.840
"Can we continue pedaling because&nbsp;
we're all paying for this class OK?"

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.160
"SHUT YOUR PIPEHOLE MISSY!"

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.560
Though this sketch isn't as iconic as Farley's&nbsp;
first foray with the motivational speaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.560
it's enough of a crowd pleaser for the audience.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.080
Following the sketch is a "Weekend Update"&nbsp;
segment of the show, hosted by Norm Macdonald.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.680
Norm's tenure on "Saturday Night&nbsp;
Live" was no stranger to controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.720
particularly following the highly-publicized&nbsp;
O.J. Simpson murder trial. Unbeknownst to Norm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.640
the Farley-hosted episode would be one of&nbsp;
his last appearances on "Weekend Update"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.480
before he was fired, though he'd&nbsp;
later return to host in 1999.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.840
As usual, Norm's jokes during&nbsp;
this episode were divisive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.920
but he was prepared when one didn't go well.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.360
"That one wasn't very good,&nbsp;
but try to laugh anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.120
because it helps Farley have a little rest."

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.880
Sadly, Norm's firing would come to pass&nbsp;
only a few months after this episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.200
According to "Live From New York," a book about&nbsp;
the oral history of "Saturday Night Live,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.400
Lorne Michaels was told to fire the "Weekend&nbsp;
Update" anchor by NBC president Don Ohlmeyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.880
at a dinner that took place the same night&nbsp;
as Farley's funeral. Norm remained an iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.720
stand-up comedian following "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;
up until his recent passing in September 2021.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.320
One of Farley's sketches later in&nbsp;
the show would come to be a historic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.240
moment for "Saturday Night Live," though&nbsp;
audiences wouldn't know it at the time.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.120
The sketch centers on a daytime talk show&nbsp;
hosted by Ana Gasteyer, with her guest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.200
played by Cheri Oteri, complaining&nbsp;
about her problems raising her baby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.920
The baby in question is predictably played&nbsp;
by Farley, who serves as a force of chaos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.120
on the show and its audience. It only gets more&nbsp;
absurd when Tim Meadows enters as a child doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.120
only to have both of his arms&nbsp;
ripped off by a manic Farley-baby.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.880
What makes this sketch such an important&nbsp;
moment for "Saturday Night Live" is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:27.360
according to Newsweek, it was the first&nbsp;
sketch written by Tina Fey to get on the air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.240
The future "30 Rock" star was a newly-hired&nbsp;
writer in the 23rd Season and would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.800
to make history on the show as head writer,&nbsp;
"Weekend Update" anchor, and six-time host.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.840
One of Chris Farley's most popular recurring&nbsp;
sketches during his time on the show was as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:47.280
member of the Chicago Super Fans. The characters&nbsp;
were often seen in some kind of pub or sports bar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.800
conversing about their favorite football team,&nbsp;
"Da Bears," and their head coach, Mike Ditka.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.760
When Farley hosted in 1997, the Super Fans&nbsp;
returned for a pre-taped sketch revolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.880
around Mike Ditka's real-life departure from the&nbsp;
Chicago Bears to join the New Orleans Saints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.120
and the Super Fans grip with their crumbling&nbsp;
lives following the loss of their beloved Ditka.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:13.920
Farley's character, Todd, has chosen to reject&nbsp;
reality and believes that it's 1986. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.360
Todd is finally able to accept reality after&nbsp;
receiving a phone call from the real Mike Ditka.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.000
It's a great final showcase for one of Farley's&nbsp;
most memorable recurring sketches. At the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.720
time, a bit where Farley's character suffers a&nbsp;
heart attack does come across as hard to watch.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.920
Despite Chris Farley's episode being a welcome&nbsp;
return for the "Saturday Night Live" legend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.240
audiences could tell that the comedian was in&nbsp;
a worsening condition. His voice throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.040
the episode was noticeably hoarse after dress&nbsp;
rehearsal, which wasn't helped by his appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.760
as characters like Matt Foley which required&nbsp;
him to strain his vocal cords even harder.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.800
In several books detailing Farley's life, like&nbsp;
"Live From New York" and "The Chris Farley Show,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:58.240
Farley's condition backstage was delved into.&nbsp;
Norm Macdonald criticized the decision to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.400
Farley on an exercise bike in the Matt&nbsp;
Foley sketch and claimed he tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.760
stretch out that episode's "Weekend&nbsp;
Update" to give him time to rest.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.120
In future reruns of the episode, the cold open&nbsp;
and monologue were cut out of respect for Farley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.160
Understandably, the episode is&nbsp;
not easy to swallow anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.040
especially for those who knew Farley well. In&nbsp;
the hours after Farley's death, "Saturday Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.320
Live" writer Al Franken stated that he had found&nbsp;
elements of the episode, quote, "disturbing."

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:30.960
On December 18, 1997, Chris Farley was found dead&nbsp;
in his Chicago apartment after overdosing on drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.080
and alcohol. Farley was only 33 at the time,&nbsp;
and yet his death wasn't a shock to his close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:41.120
friends and family. in "Live From New York,"&nbsp;
Bob Odenkirk and Chris Rock describe the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:46.720
times they saw Farley, feeling like his fate was&nbsp;
inevitable. Others, like Robert Smigel, described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:50.880
the immense guilt they felt over not being able&nbsp;
to help their friend through his struggles.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.520
In retrospect, many of Farley's collaborators&nbsp;
have expressed their distaste for Farley's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.760
career trajectory. One of Farley's&nbsp;
most famous sketches, "Chippendale's,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.600
has been criticized by former "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" writers and cast members for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.960
psychologically damaging Farley's self-image. In&nbsp;
an interview with The New Yorker, Robert Smigel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.280
regretted adding the coffee table-smashing&nbsp;
at the end of the original Matt Foley sketch.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:17.360
One person who has continued to spread&nbsp;
the good word about Chris Farley is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:21.200
Adam Sandler. The two were close-knit&nbsp;
on the cast of "Saturday Night Live,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.560
appearing together in sketches like "Herlihy Boy"&nbsp;
and "Lunch Lady Land." However, unlike Farley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:31.600
Sandler waited a long time to return to host the&nbsp;
iconic late-night sketch show. He finally made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:37.120
his hosting debut in 2019, reprising some of&nbsp;
his iconic characters like Opera Man. The real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.680
big moment of the night came at the very end of&nbsp;
the show, right before the cast said good night.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:46.720
In the late 2010s, Sandler filmed his&nbsp;
first comedy special in years, "100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:51.840
Fresh," which ends with a song Sandler sings&nbsp;
as a tribute to Farley. Sandler reprised the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.640
song on the very stage where he and Farley&nbsp;
made some of the best comedy of the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.920
despite the criticism they&nbsp;
received from NBC higher-ups.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.880
The song is equal parts&nbsp;
hilarious and heart-warming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.040
and the moment was described by the cast&nbsp;
and crew on the episode as a tearjerker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.520
for the audience and Sandler. Still, it's a&nbsp;
touching moment to remind audiences just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.560
powerful of a presence Chris Farley was and how&nbsp;
underappreciated he may have been at the time.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:21.120
If you or anyone you know is struggling&nbsp;
with addiction issues, help is available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.760
Visit the Substance Abuse&nbsp;
and Mental Health Services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:37.520
Administration website or contact SAMHSA's&nbsp;
National Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP (4357).

